  "scope_note": "A literary critic, not a historian; the entry is for a framework historians meet across fields. It rests on texts read on 2026-10-14: Orientalism in the 1978 Pantheon first edition (introduction pp. 1–28 in full; pp. 94, 153–156, 204 and 321 for passages used); The Question of Palestine (1980 Vintage reprint of the 1979 book; pp. 56–59 and 74–76); the Covering Islam introduction (p. ix); 'Orientalism Reconsidered' (1985) in full; the foreword to Selected Subaltern Studies (1988, pp. v–x) in full; and Culture and Imperialism (1993 Knopf first edition; introduction pp. xii–xxv and pp. 41–42, 51, 66, 240–245, 251 and 278). Not read: 'Traveling Theory' (The World, the Text, and the Critic, 1983) and 'Foucault and the Imagination of Power' (1986), both lending-only or closed here; his critique of Foucault is taken from Culture and Imperialism, which cites the 1986 essay as 'my critique'. He is not presented as a founder: a 1992 critique calls his blend of themes 'virtually a paradigm for a new generation of historians and anthropologists' (O'Hanlon and Washbrook, first page only) and a 1996 review calls Orientalism 'undoubtedly' the inspiration behind critical interest in 'empire, knowledge and power' (McEwan); these are readers' judgements. Contested in period by Lewis (1982) and al-'Azm (1981), both read; James Clifford's review (History and Theory, 1980), Lata Mani and Ruth Frankenberg (Economy and Society, 1985), Dennis Porter (1983) and Aijaz Ahmad (1991–92) are recorded but were not read: Porter is known here from Said's 1985 reply, Ahmad from Robert Irwin's later account (2006), Reina Lewis (1996) from McEwan's review. Works after 2000 are not drawn on; Irwin (2006) is a labelled later reading."
 },
