  {
   "proposal_id": "pe137_said_rel_02",
   "source": "foucault",
   "target": "edward_said",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "influence",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Foucault's notion of discourse used to identify Orientalism, with an explicit disagreement over the weight of individual authors (1978)",
   "map_label": "Discourse, with consequential authors, 1978",
   "evidence_note": "Said's own in-period statements. Orientalism (Pantheon, 1978; page images checked): 'I have found it useful here to employ Michel Foucault's notion of a discourse, as described by him in The Archaeology of Knowledge and in Discipline and Punish, to identify Orientalism. My contention is that without examining Orientalism as a discourse one cannot possibly understand the enormously systematic discipline by which European culture was able to manage—and even produce—the Orient politically, sociologically, militarily, ideologically, scientifically, and imaginatively during the post-Enlightenment period' (p. 3). 'Yet unlike Michel Foucault, to whose work I am greatly indebted, I do believe in the determining imprint of individual writers upon the otherwise anonymous collective body of texts constituting a discursive formation like Orientalism'; 'Foucault believes that in general the individual text or author counts for very little; empirically, in the case of Orientalism (and perhaps nowhere else) I find this not to be so' (p. 23). Elsewhere he writes of 'what Michel Foucault calls a discourse, whose material presence or weight, not the originality of a given author, is really responsible for the texts produced out of it' (p. 94), and he groups 'Gramsci, certainly, and Foucault and Raymond Williams in their very different ways' as illustrating that hegemonic culture's constraints on writers 'were productive, not unilaterally inhibiting' (p. 14). In-period criticism of the combination: al-'Azm (1981, read) objects that for Said 'representations, images, words, metaphors ... are, if not the very stuff of reality, then certainly much more important and informative substitutes for raw reality itself'. Later reading, labelled: Irwin (2006, pp. 289–290) sets the p. 23 and p. 94 passages against each other and argues that 'Foucault and Gramsci have different and contrasting notions of discourse'; Ahmad (1991–92) is reported by Irwin (p. 300) as finding that 'Said's allegiance to Foucault ... got him into the worst muddles'; Ahmad's texts were not read. The edge records Said's stated use and revision of Foucault, not a reading of Foucault's own position; Said's later criticism is edge 4.",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe137_said_orientalism_1978",
    "said_orientalism",
    "pe137_said_alazm_1981",
    "pe137_said_irwin_2006",
    "pe137_said_ahmad_in_theory_1992"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
