  {
   "proposal_id": "pe137_said_rel_03",
   "source": "gramsci",
   "target": "edward_said",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "influence",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Gramsci's hegemony and civil society used to explain the durability of Orientalism, and his 'inventory' of historical traces taken as the book's personal starting point (1978); consent for distant rule framed as 'a very Gramscian' question (1993)",
   "map_label": "Hegemony and the inventory, 1978–93",
   "evidence_note": "Said's own in-period statements. Orientalism (Pantheon, 1978; page images checked): 'Gramsci has made the useful analytic distinction between civil and political society'; culture works in civil society 'not through domination but by what Gramsci calls consent'; 'the form of this cultural leadership is what Gramsci has identified as hegemony, an indispensable concept for any understanding of cultural life in the industrial West. It is hegemony, or rather the result of cultural hegemony at work, that gives Orientalism the durability and the strength I have been speaking about so far' (pp. 6–7). Orientalism depends on 'this flexible positional superiority, which puts the Westerner in a whole series of possible relationships with the Orient without ever losing him the relative upper hand' (p. 7). 'In the Prison Notebooks Gramsci says: \"The starting-point of critical elaboration is the consciousness of what one really is ...\"'; Said notes that the English translation omits Gramsci's conclusion, 'therefore it is imperative at the outset to compile such an inventory', and calls his study 'an attempt to inventory the traces upon me, the Oriental subject' (p. 25). Culture and Imperialism (Knopf, 1993): 'The important point—a very Gramscian one—is how the national British, French, and American cultures maintained hegemony over the peripheries. How within them was consent gained and continuously consolidated for the distant rule of native peoples and territories?' (p. 51). In 1988 he described the Subaltern Studies scholars as influenced by 'many varieties of Western Marxism, Gramsci's most eminently' (foreword, Selected Subaltern Studies, p. x). Later reading, labelled: Irwin (2006, pp. 289–290) argues that 'Foucault and Gramsci had rather different ideas about the relationship between power and knowledge'. The edge records Said's stated use of Gramsci; it makes no claim about Gramsci's own Orient, which Said does not discuss in the pages read.",
