   "proposal_id": "pe137_said_rel_05",
   "source": "edward_said",
   "target": "karl",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Marx's 1853 writings on British rule in India read as an instance in which Orientalist generalisation overrode his sympathy for Asian suffering (1978), a reading contested in period as a travesty",
   "map_label": "Marx on India as Orientalist, 1978 (contested)",
   "evidence_note": "Said's own in-period statements. Orientalism (Pantheon, 1978; page images checked), pp. 153–156. Marx 'identified the notion of an Asiatic economic system in his 1853 analyses of British rule in India, and then put beside that immediately the human depredation introduced into this system by English colonial interference, rapacity, and outright cruelty' (p. 153). After quoting Marx on England as 'the unconscious tool of history' and his Goethe citation: 'Marx's economic analyses are perfectly fitted thus to a standard Orientalist undertaking, even though Marx's humanity, his sympathy for the misery of people, are clearly engaged. Yet in the end it is the Romantic Orientalist vision that wins out' (p. 154); 'The idea of regenerating a fundamentally lifeless Asia is a piece of pure Romantic Orientalism' (p. 154); 'Marx is no exception. The collective Orient was easier for him to use in illustration of a theory than existential human identities' (p. 155); 'In part, of course, Marx was concerned with vindicating his own theses on socio-economic revolution' (p. 155); Marx is 'the case by which a non-Orientalist's human engagements were first dissolved, then usurped by Orientalist generalizations' (p. 156). The introduction announces it: 'The same kind of paradox is to be found in Marx, as I try to show in this book' (p. 14). In 1985 he names Marx among the historicists whose one human history 'either culminated in or was observed from the vantage point of Europe' ('Orientalism Reconsidered', p. 101). Marx's words appear here only as Said quotes them; the 1853 articles were not read. In-period counter-critique: al-'Azm (Khamsin 1981, read): 'I think that this account of Marx's views and analyses of highly complex historical processes and situations is a travesty'; 'only Said's excessive fascination with the verbal, textual and linguistic could lead him to portray Marx's mind as somehow usurped'; Marx's explanation 'testifies to his theoretical consistency in general, and to his keen realism'. Aijaz Ahmad's criticism of the same pages ('Between Orientalism and Historicism', Studies in History 1991; In Theory, 1992) is known here only from Irwin (2006, n. 32 to p. 296), who reports that Ahmad suggests 'Marx's position on rural Indians was not significantly different from his position on rural Germans'; Ahmad was not read. Later reading, labelled: Irwin (2006, pp. 295–296) calls the treatment a travesty and asks why there is no discussion of 'the Asiatic mode of production, Oriental despotism'.",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe137_said_orientalism_1978",
    "pe137_said_reconsidered_1985",
    "pe137_said_alazm_1981",
    "pe137_said_ahmad_in_theory_1992",
    "pe137_said_irwin_2006"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "karl/forces_relations_scope",
   "target_strand_note": "forces_relations_scope ('Production relations and the scope of historical claims'; karl_marx) is the strand closest to the dispute over how far Marx's account of India generalised a European path. No karl strand covers Marx's writings on colonial India; a reviewer could prefer null.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "pe137_said_rel_06",
   "source": "edward_said",
   "target": "global",
   "type": "critique",
   "relationship_kind": "critique",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "World history (Braudel, Wallerstein, Perry Anderson, Wolf) criticised as heir to Orientalist historicism: a homogenising scheme observed from a European vantage point that avoids its own relation to imperialism (1985)",
   "map_label": "World history as historicism, 1985",
   "evidence_note": "Said's own in-period statements. 'Orientalism Reconsidered', Cultural Critique 1 (Autumn 1985), pp. 89–107, read in full (JSTOR copy). 'One of the legacies of Orientalism, and indeed one of its epistemological foundations, is historicism'; historicism 'meant that the one human history uniting humanity either culminated in or was observed from the vantage point of Europe, or the West'; out of the recuperation of what 'Eric Wolf has called people without history ... a still later disciplinary step was taken, the founding of the science of world history, whose major practitioners include Braudel, Wallerstein, Perry Anderson, and Wolf himself' (p. 101). He grants it 'greater capacity for dealing with ... the non-synchronous experiences of Europe's Other', but finds 'a fairly uniform avoidance of the relationship between European imperialism and these variously constituted, variously formed and articulated knowledges' (p. 101). 'The curious result is that the theories of accumulation on a world scale, or the capitalist world state, or lineages of absolutism depend (a) on the same displaced percipient and historicist observer who had been an Orientalist or colonial traveler three generations ago; (b) they depend also on a homogenizing and incorporating world historical scheme that assimilated non-synchronous developments, histories, cultures, and peoples to it'; 'the problem is once again historicism' (p. 102). He also rejects 're-cycling the old Marxist or world-historical rhetoric' (p. 103). In 1993 he contrasts revisionist works with 'the so-called world historians as well as the colonial Orientalists, who have tended to herd immense amounts of material under simple and all-encompassing rubrics' (Culture and Imperialism, pp. 41–42). The named historians' positions appear only as Said characterises them; no replies were looked for. The critique names practitioners mapped across several entries (Wolf on global; Wallerstein on dependency; Anderson on historicalsociology; Braudel's person entry); global is the target because he names 'the science of world history' as the object. edge_124 (post → global, Chakrabarty) stays.",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe137_said_reconsidered_1985",
    "pe137_said_culture_imperialism_1993"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": "global/capitalism",
   "target_strand_note": "capitalism (eric_wolf) holds the one named practitioner on the global roster, and Said starts from Wolf's 'people without history'. A reviewer could prefer null, since the critique is aimed at world history as a whole.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "pe137_said_rel_07",
   "source": "edward_said",
   "target": "subaltern",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "contribution",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Foreword introducing Subaltern Studies to an Anglo-American readership, with his own caution that subaltern and elite histories are 'different but overlapping and curiously interdependent territories' (1988)",
   "map_label": "Foreword: overlapping territories, 1988",
   "evidence_note": "His own in-period text. Foreword to Ranajit Guha and Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, eds., Selected Subaltern Studies (Delhi: Oxford University Press, 1988), pp. v–x, read in full (page images of a Digital Library of India scan, OCR checked against the images). He describes Guha's opening essay as 'fiercely theoretical and intellectually insurrectionary' (p. v) and the word subaltern as deriving 'from Gramsci's usage in the Prison Notebooks' (p. vi); 'rewriting Indian history today is an extension of the struggle between subaltern and elite' (p. vii). His addition: 'Yet even though his analysis is explicit on all the main issues, there is something that could perhaps be added to it here. And that is that no matter how one tried to extricate subaltern from elite histories, they are different but overlapping and curiously interdependent territories. This, I believe, is a crucial point. For if subaltern history is construed to be only a separatist enterprise ... then it runs the risk of just being a mirror opposite the writing whose tyranny it disputes' (p. viii). He places the group in 'the vast post-colonial cultural and critical effort' (p. ix) and calls its borrowing from Foucault 'the sharing of a paradigm, rather than slavish copying' (p. x); the volume is 'a crossing of boundaries, a smuggling of ideas across lines' (p. x). In 1985 he had already listed 'the group around Ranajit Guha (Subaltern Studies)' among anti-Orientalist projects ('Orientalism Reconsidered', pp. 104–105). A contribution of framing and reception to the series' Anglo-American appearance; it does not make him a member of the collective, a founder, or the source of its theory (the existing roster row says the same). edge_115 (subaltern → post) stays.",
   "source_ids": [
    "said_subaltern_foreword_1988",
    "pe137_said_reconsidered_1985"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
   "target_strand_note": "No subaltern strand covers the series' reception or framing; popular_politics (Guha) is the strand his foreword discusses, but he is not part of it. A reviewer could add a reception strand.",
   "roster_row": "subaltern",
   "include": true
  },
  {
   "proposal_id": "pe137_said_rel_08",
   "source": "edward_said",
   "target": "anticolonial",
   "type": "connection",
   "relationship_kind": "contribution",
   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "Culture and Imperialism's account of resistance culture: anticolonial histories such as James's The Black Jacobins and Antonius's The Arab Awakening read as a 'voyage in' to metropolitan culture, alongside post-independence critiques by Guha and Alatas (1993)",
   "map_label": "Resistance culture and the voyage in, 1993",
   "evidence_note": "His own in-period text. Culture and Imperialism (Knopf, 1993), chapter 3, 'Resistance and Opposition'. He distinguishes liberal anti-colonialism, which did 'not dispute the fundamental superiority of Western man', from anti-imperialism, and argues 'there was no overall condemnation of imperialism until—and this is my point—after native uprisings were too far gone to be ignored or defeated' (pp. 240–241). Anti-imperialist work by writers from the peripheries in the metropolis is 'usually an extension into the metropolis of large-scale mass movements'; 'There is thus an overlap and interdependence that cannot theoretically be described as only the reactive assertion of a separate colonial or native identity'; 'The voyage in, then, constitutes an especially interesting variety of hybrid cultural work' (p. 244). He reads 'C.L.R. James's The Black Jacobins, published in 1938, and, appearing at almost the same time, George Antonius's The Arab Awakening' as works 'intended as serious works of scholarship and advocacy addressed from within a national movement for independence to a general audience', set against Ranajit Guha's A Rule of Property for Bengal (1963) and S. H. Alatas's The Myth of the Lazy Native (1977), which are 'post-colonial and specialist' (p. 245); 'history for Guha is critique, not the dutiful replication of colonialist objects, ideologies, and arguments' (p. 251). He also treats Fanon as one who 'programmatically seeks to treat colonial and metropolitan societies together' (p. 278). The introduction frames the chapter: 'What I left out of Orientalism was that response to Western dominance which culminated in the great movement of decolonization' (p. xii). Read: pp. xii, 240–245, 251, 278; the rest of the chapter (including the Fanon and Cabral sections) was not read closely. No in-period review assessing this chapter for historians was read. A contribution to how anticolonial histories are read and placed, not a claim that he wrote anticolonial history in their sense; The Question of Palestine (1979) is mentioned in the node, not used for this edge.",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe137_said_culture_imperialism_1993"
   ],
   "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
   "review_status": "needs_review",
   "target_strand": null,
   "target_strand_note": "The chapter crosses several anticolonial strands: enslaved_revolution (James), colonial_subject (Fanon) and culture_liberation (Cabral). No single strand fits a reading of the tradition as a whole; null.",
   "roster_row": null,
   "include": true,
   "include_note": "The weakest of the eight edges: it rests on one book's chapter and has no in-period reception from historians. A reviewer may prefer to drop it, or to reverse it as an influence from clr_james (Said reads The Black Jacobins at length)."
  }
