{
  "proposal_id": "pe137_guha_rel_02",
  "source": "ranajit_guha",
  "target": "nationaltradition",
  "type": "critique",
  "relationship_kind": "critique",
  "directed": true,
  "relationship": "Colonialist and bourgeois-nationalist histories of India criticised as two forms of elitism that write nationalism as 'a sort of spiritual biography of the Indian elite' and leave out popular initiative (1982), extended to the shared liberal assumption that colonial dominance was hegemonic (1989)",
  "map_label": "Against elitist histories of Indian nationalism, 1982–89",
  "evidence_note": "His own in-period statements. 'On Some Aspects of the Historiography of Colonial India' (1982; Selected Subaltern Studies reprint, 1988, pp. 37–44, read): 'The historiography of Indian nationalism has for a long time been dominated by elitism—colonialist elitism and bourgeois-nationalist elitism'; both 'share the prejudice that the making of the Indian nation and the development of the consciousness—nationalism—which informed this process were exclusively or predominantly elite achievements' (p. 37). The colonialist kind treats nationalism as 'a sort of “learning process”' driven by 'the expectation of rewards' (p. 38); the nationalist kind writes it up 'as a sort of spiritual biography of the Indian elite' (p. 38). Neither can explain 'the anti-Rowlatt upsurge of 1919 and the Quit India movement of 1942' as 'popular initiative asserting itself ... in defiance or absence of elite control' (p. 39). The qualification he keeps: 'Elitist historiography is of course not without its uses' (p. 38), and 'even the elitists have a part to play if only by way of teaching by negative examples' (p. 43). 'The Prose of Counter-Insurgency' (Subaltern Studies II, 1983, read): in bourgeois-nationalist historiography 'it is an elite consciousness which is read into all peasant movements as their motive force', producing 'such grotesqueries as the characterization of the Indigo Rebellion of 1860 as “the first non-violent mass movement”' (p. 38). 'Dominance without Hegemony and Its Historiography' (Subaltern Studies VI, 1989, pp. 210–309; pp. 210–215, 227–233, 291–292 and 299–303 read): 'There was one Indian battle that Britain never won. It was a battle for appropriation of the Indian past' (p. 210); 'History became thus a game for two to play as the alien colonialist project of appropriation was matched by an indigenous nationalist project of counter-appropriation' (p. 212); both rest on 'a fundamental agreement between the Indian bourgeoisie and the British' from 'the standpoint of liberalism' (p. 212); 'dominance under colonial conditions has quite erroneously been endowed with hegemony' (p. 228). On the Cambridge historians: 'The subject of this neo-colonialist historiography is the Empire' (p. 292); Anil Seal's 'general explanation' is 'a kind of academic confidence trick' (p. 300). In-period criticism of the stance: Sarkar (1997, p. 92, read) sees 'an unnoticed drift implicit even in Guha's initial formulation of the project in Subaltern Studies I': 'The “historiography of colonial India” somehow slides quickly into that of Indian nationalism', and the central problematic 'ultimately becomes “the historic failure of the nation to come into its own”' (quoting Subaltern Studies I, pp. 2–3, 7). Later reading, labelled: Chakrabarty (2000, p. 14) describes the series as starting 'as a critique of two contending schools of history: the Cambridge school and that of the nationalist historians'. This edge carries the story now told by edge_604 (subaltern → nationaltradition), whose relabel is proposed; edge_114 (subaltern → anticolonial) is discussed under edge_revisions.",
  "source_ids": [
    "guha_historiography",
    "pe137_guha_prose_1983",
    "pe137_guha_dominance_1989",
    "sarkar_decline",
    "pe137_chakrabarty_nepantla_2000"
  ],
  "classification_basis": "editorial_review",
  "review_status": "needs_review",
  "target_strand": "nationaltradition/national_exclusions",
  "target_strand_note": "national_exclusions ('Nationalist exclusions and alternative scales'; 'Guha and Chatterjee question elite and modular national accounts') holds him with Chatterjee and Ogot and matches the checked 1982 argument.",
  "roster_row": "nationaltradition",
  "include": true
}
{
  "proposal_id": "pe137_guha_rel_03",
  "source": "ranajit_guha",
  "target": "political",
  "type": "connection",
  "relationship_kind": "contribution",
  "directed": true,
  "relationship": "Peasant insurgency in colonial India read as deliberate political action against a composite dominance of landlord, moneylender and state, whose limits define 'the quality of that politics' rather than denying it (1983)",
  "map_label": "Insurgency as politics, 1983",
