  "description": "A literary theorist and the translator of Derrida’s Of Grammatology (1976), whose essays on colonial India became reference points for historians. In ‘Subaltern Studies: Deconstructing Historiography’ (1985) she read the collective ‘from within but against the grain’, proposing that its recovery of subaltern consciousness be read ‘as a strategic use of positivist essentialism in a scrupulously visible political interest’, and argued that it overlooked ‘how important the concept-metaphor woman is’ to its own discourse. ‘Can the Subaltern Speak?’ (1988) separates representation as ‘speaking for’ from ‘re-presentation’, argues that in Foucault and Deleuze’s conversation intellectuals, ‘representing them, ... represent themselves as transparent’, and reads the British abolition of sati through the sentence ‘White men are saving brown women from brown men’, set against the nativist ‘The women actually wanted to die’: between them ‘One never encounters the testimony of the women’s voice-consciousness.’ She later called the essay’s closing ‘the subaltern cannot speak!’ ‘an inadvisable remark’ (1999). In period, Gyan Prakash (1992) held that she ‘very correctly marks the silencing of the subaltern woman’ as a limit of historical understanding, Rosalind O’Hanlon (1988) urged hesitation ‘before accepting’ her proposal to re-inscribe the series’ strategy ‘as a strategy for our times’, and Terry Eagleton (1999) found her Critique ‘too well-mannered, as well as too episodic’ to be the critique its title promised.",
  "representative_figures_and_works": "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak (b. 1942); translator’s preface to Jacques Derrida, Of Grammatology (1976); “Subaltern Studies: Deconstructing Historiography” (Subaltern Studies IV, 1985; introduction to Selected Subaltern Studies, 1988); “Three Women’s Texts and a Critique of Imperialism” (Critical Inquiry, 1985); “Can the Subaltern Speak?” (in Nelson and Grossberg, eds., Marxism and the Interpretation of Culture, 1988; revised in A Critique of Postcolonial Reason, 1999); “The New Subaltern: A Silent Interview” (2000). Readings: O’Hanlon (1988); Chakrabarty (1992); Prakash (1992); Sarkar (1994); Pandey (1995); Eagleton (1999); Chatterjee (2010).",
