ADDENDUM said_orientalism {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read for the Said person entry (postcolonial batch). The PDF (57 pp.; title 'Said-Introduction and Chapter 1 of Orientalism') is a retyping of the Routledge & Kegan Paul 1978/1980 printing, which shares the Pantheon pagination (contents: Introduction 1, chapter 1 at 31, Notes 329). Its page breaks follow the book's, so book page = PDF page − 8: the earlier note's PDF pp. 11, 14–15 and 31–32 are book pp. 3, 6–7 and 23–24. Checked against the page images of the Pantheon first edition (pe137_said_orientalism_1978). The transcription has typing errors (for example 'employ is a Foucault’s notion'); quotations in the Said entry follow the scan."}
ADDENDUM said_subaltern_foreword_1988 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Foreword read in full, pp. v–x, in page images of Selected Subaltern Studies (Delhi: Oxford University Press, 1988; title page 'Edited by Ranajit Guha and Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak') from the Digital Library of India scan on the Internet Archive (item dli.bengal.10689.12740), OCR checked against the images. Passages used: Guha's essay 'fiercely theoretical and intellectually insurrectionary' (p. v); subaltern from 'Gramsci's usage in the Prison Notebooks' (p. vi); 'an extension of the struggle between subaltern and elite' (p. vii); subaltern and elite histories as 'different but overlapping and curiously interdependent territories' and the risk of a 'separatist enterprise' becoming 'a mirror opposite' (p. viii); 'the vast post-colonial cultural and critical effort' (p. ix); influence of 'Western Marxism, Gramsci's most eminently', and of Foucault as 'the sharing of a paradigm, rather than slavish copying'; 'a crossing of boundaries, a smuggling of ideas across lines' (p. x). This edition is the Delhi printing; the New York OUP printing recorded in the original note was not compared."}
ADDENDUM guha_historiography {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read in full for the Guha person entry, including the Preface (Selected Subaltern Studies reprint pp. 35–36; dated Canberra, August 1981). Quoted: 'rectify the elitist bias'; 'of inferior rank'; subordination 'expressed in terms of class, caste, age, gender and office'; Gramsci's six-point project and 'subaltern groups are always subject to the activity of ruling groups, even when they rebel and rise up' (p. 35); 'colonialist elitism and bourgeois-nationalist elitism' (p. 37); 'spiritual biography of the Indian elite' and 'not without its uses' (p. 38); 'the contribution made by the people on their own' (p. 39); 'the politics of the people', 'an autonomous domain', 'As modern as indigenous elite politics' (p. 40); 'the failure of the Indian bourgeoisie to speak for the nation' (p. 41); 'a great deal of overlap' (p. 42); 'Let a hundred flowers blossom'; 'We claim no more than to try and indicate an orientation' (p. 43); the note on terms (p. 44). Note 1 thanks Gautam Bhadra, Dipesh Chakrabarty and Raghabendra Chattopadhyay. Reprint pages; Subaltern Studies I pagination (pp. 1–8) is cited by Sarkar and O'Hanlon."}
ADDENDUM guha_elementary {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Primary text now read for the Guha person entry. Introduction pp. 1–17 OCR'd at 300 dpi from a scan of the Duke University Press 1999 printing (foreword by James C. Scott) hosted by Berhampore Girls' College; preface dated Canberra, January 1983; the text keeps the 1983 Oxford pagination, checked against the Digital Library of India scan of the Delhi 1983 edition (Internet Archive dli.bengal.10689.12632; imprint 'First published 1983, Second impression 1986'). Quoted: 'Insurgency was thus the necessary antithesis of colonialism' (p. 2); 'a discourse of power'; the peasant 'denied recognition as a subject of history' (p. 3); 'a consciousness'; spontaneity 'elitist as well as erroneous' (p. 4); Gramsci on spontaneity; Hobsbawm's 'pioneering work' and 'pre-political' (p. 5); 'helps little in understanding the experience of colonial India'; 'nothing ... that was not political' (p. 6); 'a composite apparatus of dominance' (p. 8); 'negative and inversive procedure' (p. 9); limits that define 'the quality of that politics' (p. 10); 'inchoate and naive' (p. 11); Gramsci's 'pillars of politics' and 'elementary aspects' (p. 12); 'its sovereignty, its consistency and its logic' (p. 13); reading rebel consciousness in counter-insurgency records (pp. 14–15). Bibliography: Gramsci, Selections (1971); Hobsbawm, Primitive Rebels (Manchester, 1959); Hobsbawm and Rudé, Captain Swing (1969). Pp. 89–90 (Thompson on the Blacks; Hobsbawm's 'social banditry') read in the 1983 edition's text. Scott's foreword: Elementary Aspects 'became, in effect, the founding document' of 'the Subaltern Studies School' (p. ix). The rest of the book is unread."}
ADDENDUM sarkar_decline {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read for the Guha person entry: pp. 82–87 and 90–94 OCR'd, pp. 84–87 checked against the page images. Quoted: Guha's 1982 preface and essay 'full of references to “subaltern classes”, evocations of Gramsci' (p. 82); British Marxian social history 'probably the most significant' influence and Guha's 'subaltern' used 'somewhat in the way Thompson deployed the term “plebeian”' (p. 83; n. 3: Elementary Aspects 'frequently cited Thompson with approval', to Whigs and Hunters and Albion's Fatal Tree); the early volumes 'largely ignored in the West' (p. 84; n. 4 names O'Hanlon 1988 as Modern Asian Studies' first engagement); 'argued with considerable justice by Guha and other contributors' (p. 85; n. 7: 'A more explicit critique of orthodox Marxist historiography was made by Guha' in 'The Prose of Counterinsurgency'); 'over-flamboyant' paradigm claims and the group's modification of 'the historiography of anticolonial nationalism' (p. 86); 'Guha's attempt to use some of the language and methods of Lévi-Straussian structuralism' (p. 87); Chatterjee's 'derivative discourses' (p. 91); 'an unnoticed drift implicit even in Guha's initial formulation' (p. 92); Guha's 'Chandra's Death' as a 'seldom-referred-to' alternative (pp. 93–94)."}
ADDENDUM gramsci {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Checked for the Guha person entry: 'Subaltern groups are always subject to the activity of ruling groups, even when they rebel and rise up' ('Notes on Italian History', p. 55), the sentence Guha's 1982 preface quotes; and 'Elements of Politics', p. 144: the 'first elements' which, repeated, 'become the pillars of politics and of any collective action whatsoever', which Elementary Aspects cites (Gramsci: 144)."}
ADDENDUM spivak_speak {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read for the Spivak person entry: the reprint's pp. 66–104 in full and notes pp. 104–111. The copy is the reprint in Patrick Williams and Laura Chrisman, eds., Colonial Discourse and Post-Colonial Theory (1993), where it is chapter 4 at pp. 66–111 and its opening is abridged ('[ ... ]'); its first page cites the 1988 Nelson and Grossberg pagination, pp. 271–313. Two 1988 page anchors were found in other texts: the 'feeling of community' passage (reprint p. 72) is 1988 p. 277 (Chakrabarty, 'Artifice', 1992, n. 48), and the closing 'The subaltern cannot speak' (reprint p. 104) is 1988 p. 308 (Sunder Rajan, in Morris, ed., 2010). The reprint's last lines are OCR-damaged; their wording was checked against the unpaginated 1988 text reprinted in Morris (2010). Pages used: p. 66 (the subject of the West); p. 70 (the two senses of representation; intellectuals 'represent themselves as transparent'); p. 71 ('an essentialist, utopian politics'); pp. 76–78 (epistemic violence; the question); p. 79 (Guha's autonomy: 'I cannot entirely endorse'); pp. 82–84 (the female subaltern 'even more deeply in shadow'); pp. 85–86 and 90 (Foucault); pp. 87–89 and 104 (Derrida); p. 90 ('information retrieval'); pp. 92–97 and 102 (sati); p. 104 (close); n. 47 p. 108 ('the critique of imperialism is deconstruction as such'); n. 65 p. 109 (Lata Mani)."}
ADDENDUM derrida_grammatology_corrected {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Translator's preface re-read for the Spivak person entry (preface pp. ix–lxxxvii per the contents page; corrected-edition pagination). Quoted: 'To locate the promising marginal text, to disclose the undecidable moment, to pry it loose with the positive lever of the signifier; to reverse the resident hierarchy, only to displace it; to dismantle in order to reconstitute what is always already inscribed. Deconstruction in a nutshell' (p. lxxvii). Whether the 1976 first edition paginates the preface identically was not checked."}
ADDENDUM pe132_chakrabarty_artifice_1992 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read for the Spivak person entry. Quoted: 'in Gayatri Spivak’s words', 'the feeling of community that belongs to national links and political organizations' (p. 18; n. 48 cites 'Can the Subaltern Speak?', 1988, p. 277); the antihistorical subject 'cannot speak itself as “theory”' and, 'Much like Spivak’s “subaltern”', 'can only be spoken for and spoken of by the transition narrative' (p. 19; n. 52 cites the 1988 essay and her 1990 Socialist Review interview). Spivak is thanked in the unnumbered first note (p. 23) and credited in n. 43 (p. 25)."}
ADDENDUM pe137_spivak_deconstructing_1985 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read in full for the Spivak person entry (same DLI scan; pages from its page-number file and running heads). Pages added to those in the guha draft: 'plotted as confrontations rather than transition' (p. 330); Chakrabarty's labour essays discussed (pp. 331, 344); 'This is why I hope to align them with deconstruction' and 'the greatest gift of deconstruction' (p. 336); the strategy 'might be re-inscribed as a strategy for our times' (p. 345); 'No such luck for the “female”' (p. 357); 'Male subaltern and historian are here united' (p. 358); 'woman is the neglected syntagm' (p. 359); the 'red herring' sentence (p. 360); 'not a task that the group should fairly be asked to perform' (p. 361); 'Envoi' (p. 363)."}
ADDENDUM pe137_ohanlon_recovering_1988 {'status': 'supporting_page_checked', 'checked_on': '2026-10-14', 'note': "Re-read for the Spivak person entry (Verso reprint pagination). Quoted: 'This, then, I think, is what Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak means when she speaks of the contributors’ use of “a strategic use of positivist essentialism in a scrupulously visible political interest”' (p. 80; n. 14, p. 113, cites SS IV p. 342), and the strategy's 'larger significance and, more importantly, its limitations, have yet to receive any proper public discussion' (p. 80); 'we should certainly hesitate before accepting Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak’s suggestion that the strategy of the Subaltern series “in claiming a positive subject-position for the subaltern might be re-inscribed as a strategy for our times”' (p. 105; n. 83, p. 115, cites SS IV p. 345)."}

