{"id": "edge_124", "source": "post", "target": "global", "relationship": "Chakrabarty’s historical-difference argument tests Europe-centered assumptions in global narratives", "type": "critique", "relationship_kind": "critique", "directed": true, "source_ids": ["post", "global"], "evidence_note": "Postcolonial criticism tests global narratives for Eurocentric assumptions; it does not reject all transregional inquiry. This is a conceptual critique mapped editorially to global history; the checked introduction does not document reception by every global historian.", "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "map_label": "Testing universal historical paths"}
{"id": "edge_159", "source": "gramsci", "target": "subaltern", "relationship": "Gramsci’s concepts of subaltern groups and hegemony are adapted within Subaltern Studies", "type": "connection", "directed": true, "relationship_kind": "influence", "source_ids": ["gramsci", "subaltern", "sarkar_decline", "spivak_speak"], "evidence_note": "Guha’s adaptation does not make the changing Subaltern Studies collective a uniform Gramscian project. Sarkar’s primary retrospective p. 82 identifies Gramsci in the early volumes; Spivak pp. 78–79 critically discusses the conceptual passage to colonial India. The original methodological criteria52–55 specify organization, autonomy and interrupted activity; they do not define subaltern groups as simply passive or without voice.", "map_label": "Gramsci adapted to colonial historical problems"}
