{"id": "edge_917", "source": "dipesh_chakrabarty", "target": "eric_hobsbawm", "relationship": "Hobsbawm's category 'prepolitical', as quoted in Provincializing Europe, criticised as an instance of the historicism Western Marxism cultivated: the critique Guha began (1983), extended by Chakrabarty (2000)", "type": "critique", "relationship_kind": "critique", "directed": true, "source_ids": ["post", "pe132_hobsbawm_primitive_rebels", "pe132_chakrabarty_universalism_2000", "pe132_chakrabarty_artifice_1992", "guha_elementary"], "evidence_note": "His own text. Provincializing Europe (2000), introduction pp. 11–15 read in the publisher's excerpt: 'Hobsbawm's category “prepolitical” revealed the limits of how far historicist Marxist thought could go in responding to the challenge posed to European political thought by the entry of the peasant into the modern sphere of politics' (p. 11). Hobsbawm 'readily admitted that it was the “acquisition of political consciousness” by peasants that “made our century the most revolutionary in history”', yet 'missed the implications of this observation for the historicism that already underlay his own analysis' (p. 11). Chakrabarty quotes Hobsbawm calling peasants 'pre-political people who have not yet found, or only begun to find, a specific language in which to express themselves. [Capitalism] comes to them from outside, insidiously by the operation of economic forces which they do not understand', and writes that in 'Hobsbawm's historicist language, the social movements of the peasants of the twentieth century remained “archaic”' (p. 12). Hobsbawm's words are given here only as Chakrabarty quotes them (a quoted-in witness; the bracketed '[Capitalism]' is in Chakrabarty's text). 'The analytical impulse of Hobsbawm's study belongs to a variety of historicism that Western Marxism has cultivated since its inception', including the language of 'survivals' and 'remnants' and 'variations on the theme of “uneven development”' (p. 12). The work quoted: the 1.132 adversarial review, reading a transcription of the book's notes, reports that note 36 (p. 261) cites Hobsbawm, Primitive Rebels (Manchester, 1978 edition; first published 1959), pp. 2–3, and that note 35 concerns rational-choice theory, not Hobsbawm. Neither the notes nor Hobsbawm's pages were read for this revision: the publisher's excerpt omits the notes, the transcription and the open OCR copies of Primitive Rebels were blocked or truncated, and the Internet Archive copies are borrow-only. The identification is therefore the reviewer's, and Hobsbawm's own wording is unverified here. The review also reports, from an OCR witness, that Hobsbawm's p. 2 speaks of the participants' aspirations where Chakrabarty's quotation has 'express themselves', and that Hobsbawm treats the movements of these pre-political people as consequential, not marginal; his book studies a selection of 'archaic forms of social movement' (its subtitle). The edge therefore records Chakrabarty's critique of the category as he presents it, not a verified reading of Hobsbawm's argument. He credits the critique to Guha: Subaltern Studies registered 'a deep sense of unease with the very idea of the “political”' in English-language Marxist historiography, 'Nowhere ... more visible than in Ranajit Guha's criticism of the British historian Eric Hobsbawm's category “prepolitical” in his 1983 book Elementary Aspects of Peasant Insurgency' (p. 11). He keeps Marx: 'Marx is critical for the enterprise' (p. 18), and in 1992 Marx 'remains ... a relevant and fundamental critic of both capitalism and liberalism' ('Artifice', p. 4). The same argument appears in Public Culture (2000, p. 670): read historically, Marx's 'not yet' 'refers us back to the idea of history as a waiting room ... the period to which the Third World is often consigned'. No in-period reply by Hobsbawm was looked for or read.", "classification_basis": "editorial_review", "map_label": "Against the 'prepolitical', 2000", "proposal_id": "pe132_chakrabarty_rel_04"}
karl Modes of production, relations of exploitation, class struggle, and historically specific capitalism. Later Marxists disagree over determinism, agency, and culture. Marx’s 1859 developmental propositions coexist with his 1877 rejection of a universal path. Later debates concern political action, imperial accumulation, dialectical and analytical explanation, and reproduction of material life. Marx’s historical materialism must be distinguished from the competing historical programmes of later Marxists. Neither Marx’s late qualification nor Engels’s acknowledgment of political effects cancels their stronger causal claims. Lukács, Luxemburg, Lenin, Althusser, Cohen and feminist reworkings offer distinct arguments; shared materialist resources do not establish one doctrine or a verified world chronology.
nationaltradition National and imperial histories join source work to competing accounts of peoples, states, liberty and civilization. Michelet, Macaulay and Bancroft differ in method and purpose; colonial hierarchies, liberation histories and critiques of nationalist exclusions remain in tension. A teaching umbrella, not a single national or imperial doctrine. Cultural and documentary inquiry coexist with narratives of progress. Colonial policy, anticolonial histories, subaltern criticism and collective African projects have different aims; no universal nationalist origin or automatic emancipation is implied.
political Examines institutions, diplomacy and struggles over power through different methods. Namier reconstructs parliamentary connections; Benson studies voters; Tilly investigates collective claims; Hunt joins political symbols and social identities. Hill, Dorothy Thompson and Flexner examine radical and rights politics, while Guha and Cooper investigate insurgency and labor under colonial rule. These approaches overlap without replacing all earlier political history. Diplomatic, institutional, social, and cultural political histories persist and interact; the field did not disappear with the rise of social history. Distinguish institutional elites, electoral categories, popular organization and colonial subjects. Cultural political history can retain social and quantitative evidence; attention to popular action does not make all movements democratic. Regional examples are selective, not a globally complete political canon.
anticolonial Recasts empire through slavery, resistance, liberation, and colonized peoples’ historical agency. Caribbean, African, and other projects develop through different political struggles and intellectual traditions; historical scholarship and theories of decolonization overlap without forming one school. James, Williams, Fanon, and Rodney make different arguments. Colonial subjects and liberation politics do not establish one school or Marxist identity. Césaire’s polemic and Cabral’s political theory add distinct questions; cultural struggle and economic explanation can interact. The present Caribbean/African selection does not exhaust anti-colonial historical writing in Asia or elsewhere. Naoroji adds one Indian precursor through a specific1901argument; his use of Mill distinguishes theoretical borrowing from acceptance of colonial policy.
global Moves across national containers through comparison, interaction, circulation, and connections. These approaches differ; global scale need not imply one theory of globalization. World synthesis, comparative development, capitalism, and connected histories differ in units, scales, and explanatory assumptions.

