{"id": "pe137_said_covering_islam_1981", "title": "Edward W. Said, Covering Islam: How the Media and the Experts Determine How We See the Rest of the World (New York: Pantheon, 1981).", "url": "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/159777/covering-islam-by-edward-w-said/", "citation": "Edward W. Said, Covering Islam (New York: Pantheon Books, 1981).", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Internet Archive item covering-islam (ABBYY OCR of a scan without its imprint page). Its contents (Introduction ix; chapters on 'Islam as News', 'The Iran Story', 'Knowledge and Power'; Index 177) and an introduction that opens 'This is the third and last in a series of books', with no 1997 'Introduction to the Vintage Edition', match the 1981 edition; the publisher and edition are inferred, not seen. Only the introduction's first page was read. Passage used: 'The underlying theme of Orientalism is the affiliation of knowledge with power' (p. ix)."}, "scope_note": "Node and edge 1: his own 1981 summary of Orientalism's theme."}
{"id": "pe137_said_culture_imperialism_1993", "title": "Edward W. Said, Culture and Imperialism (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1993).", "url": "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/159778/culture-and-imperialism-by-edward-w-said/", "citation": "Edward W. Said, Culture and Imperialism (New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 1993; first edition).", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Internet Archive item cultureimperialism2 (file cultureimperiali00edwa.pdf; scan with running heads; imprint 'Copyright © 1993 by Edward W. Said ... Published in the United States by Alfred A. Knopf ... First Edition'). Read: introduction pp. xii–xiv and xxv; pp. 26, 41–42, 51, 66, 240–245, 251, 278 and note 198 (in the notes section). Passages used: 'What I left out of Orientalism' (p. xii); 'two historiographies, one linear and subsuming, the other contrapuntal and often nomadic' (p. xxv); Foucault and Williams, 'the imperial experience is quite irrelevant' (p. 41); 'so-called world historians' (p. 42); 'not univocally but contrapuntally'; 'a very Gramscian one' (p. 51); 'contrapuntal reading must take account of both processes' (p. 66); anti-colonialism and anti-imperialism (pp. 240–241); 'The voyage in' (p. 244); James, Antonius, Guha and Alatas (p. 245); 'history for Guha is critique' (p. 251); Foucault and Fanon, 'microphysics of power', 'swerves away from politics entirely', Frankfurt School silence (p. 278); n. 198 citing 'Foucault and the Imagination of Power' (1986) as 'my critique'. The rest of the book, including the Austen, Kipling, Camus and Verdi readings, is unread."}, "scope_note": "In-period book: contrapuntal reading, resistance culture, and his critique of Foucault."}
{"id": "pe137_said_exchange_nyrb_1982", "title": "Edward W. Said, Oleg Grabar and Bernard Lewis, “Orientalism: An Exchange”, New York Review of Books (12 August 1982).", "url": "https://www.nybooks.com/articles/1982/08/12/orientalism-an-exchange/", "citation": "Edward W. Said and Oleg Grabar, letters, with a reply by Bernard Lewis, “Orientalism: An Exchange”, The New York Review of Books, 12 August 1982.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full on the NYRB website (web text, no page numbers). Passages used: Said, 'It is idiotic to say that Orientalism is a conspiracy or to suggest that \"the West\" is evil'; its 'modern discourse is correctly perceived as a discourse of power'; Geertz's 'rather trivial arguments against me'; the omission of German scholars explained. Lewis, 'It is difficult to argue with a scream of rage'; 'Mr. Said seems to be unable to conceive that there may be intellectual disagreements which are not political in origin or purpose'; 'The tragedy of Mr. Said’s Orientalism is that it takes a genuine problem of real importance, and reduces it to the level of political polemic and personal abuse.' Grabar's letter was not used."}, "scope_note": "In-period exchange: Said's reply to Lewis and Lewis's rejoinder."}
{"id": "pe137_said_irwin_2006", "title": "Robert Irwin, Dangerous Knowledge: Orientalism and Its Discontents (Woodstock and New York: Overlook Press, 2006).", "url": "https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/25791/for-lust-of-knowing-by-robert-irwin/9780140289237", "citation": "Robert Irwin, Dangerous Knowledge: Orientalism and Its Discontents (Overlook Press, 2006); published in Britain as For Lust of Knowing: The Orientalists and Their Enemies (Allen Lane, 2006).", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in an Internet Archive upload of the Overlook edition (imprint: 'This edition first published in the United States in 2006 by The Overlook Press'; copyright 2006 Robert Irwin); pages from running heads. Read: the opening summary of Orientalism and pp. 289–290, 295–296, 299–300 and n. 32. Passages used: Foucault and Gramsci 'have different and contrasting notions of discourse' (pp. 289–290); Marx's writings 'travestied' and the missing 'Asiatic mode of production, Oriental despotism' (pp. 295–296); 'Early reviews of Orientalism were mostly hostile' (p. 299); al-'Azm and Ahmad (pp. 299–300); n. 32 on Ahmad and al-'Azm. The British title is from secondary knowledge and was not checked. A later (2006) and polemical reading; cited only as labelled later reading."}, "scope_note": "Later reading (2006): an Orientalist historian's critique of Said; not in-period evidence."}
{"id": "pe137_said_lewis_nyrb_1982", "title": "Bernard Lewis, “The Question of Orientalism”, New York Review of Books (24 June 1982).", "url": "https://www.nybooks.com/articles/1982/06/24/the-question-of-orientalism/", "citation": "Bernard Lewis, “The Question of Orientalism”, The New York Review of Books, 24 June 1982.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full on the NYRB website (web text, no page numbers; volume and issue not recorded). Passages used: the term 'emptied of its previous content and given an entirely new one'; 'To prove this point, Mr. Said makes a number of very arbitrary decisions. His Orient is reduced to the Middle East, and his Middle East to a part of the Arab world'; the omission of the Germans; 'a remarkably arbitrary choice of works'; Said's attitude 'far more negative than that of the most arrogant European imperialist writers'; 'The most rigorous and penetrating critique of Orientalist scholarship has always been and will remain that of the Orientalists themselves.'"}, "scope_note": "In-period critique by the leading Orientalist Said criticised."}
{"id": "pe137_said_mani_frankenberg_1985", "title": "Lata Mani and Ruth Frankenberg, “The Challenge of Orientalism”, Economy and Society 14, no. 2 (1985): 174–192.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1080/03085148500000009", "citation": "Lata Mani and Ruth Frankenberg, “The Challenge of Orientalism”, Economy and Society 14:2 (1985): 174–192. doi:10.1080/03085148500000009.", "verification": {"status": "bibliographic_metadata_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Metadata only: Crossref record (Economy and Society 14:2, April 1985, pp. 174–192; authors Mani and Frankenberg). Closed access; not read. No claim about its argument is made."}, "scope_note": "Recorded in-period critics; unread."}
{"id": "pe137_said_mcewan_1996", "title": "Cheryl McEwan, “Gender, culture and imperialism” (review article on Reina Lewis, Gendering Orientalism, and Anne McClintock, Imperial Leather), Journal of Historical Geography 22, no. 4 (1996): 489–494.", "url": "https://dept.english.wisc.edu/amcclintock/writing/JHG_review.pdf", "citation": "Cheryl McEwan, “Gender, culture and imperialism”, review article, Journal of Historical Geography 22:4 (1996): 489–494.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in a PDF of the journal pages (running head 'Journal of Historical Geography, 22, 4 (1996) 489–494'; Academic Press 1996) hosted on a University of Wisconsin faculty page. Passages used: 'The inspiration behind the critical interest in empire, knowledge and power was undoubtedly Edward Said's groundbreaking and provocative Orientalism'; feminist scholars 'argue that the critics of Orientalism and ethnocentric scholarship continue to write out gender' (p. 489); Reina Lewis's book 'a response to Said in its efforts to destabilize the homogenizing tendencies of twentieth-century critiques of Orientalism' (p. 490); 'alternative orientalisms which are themselves fragmented, fluid and contestable' (p. 491). Reina Lewis's book itself was not read (Internet Archive copy lending-only)."}, "scope_note": "In-period historical-geography review: Said's reception and the feminist critique (Reina Lewis, 1996)."}
{"id": "pe137_said_orientalism_1978", "title": "Edward W. Said, Orientalism (New York: Pantheon Books, 1978).", "url": "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/159783/orientalism-by-edward-w-said/", "citation": "Edward W. Said, Orientalism (New York: Pantheon Books, 1978; first edition). Copy read: Internet Archive item orientalism-edward-said (file orientalism0000said_r4m0.pdf, scan of the Pantheon first edition).", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Page images and text layer of a scan of the Pantheon first edition (copyright page: 'Copyright © 1978 by Edward W. Said ... Published in the United States by Pantheon Books ... First Edition'). Running heads give book pages. Read: introduction pp. 1–28 in full; pp. 94, 153–156, 204 and 321. Passages used: 'The Orient was almost a European invention' (p. 1); 'a style of thought based upon an ontological and epistemological distinction' (p. 2); 'a Western style for dominating, restructuring, and having authority over the Orient'; Foucault's 'notion of a discourse, as described by him in The Archaeology of Knowledge and in Discipline and Punish' (p. 3); 'a creation with no corresponding reality'; 'a complex hegemony' (p. 5); Gramsci's civil and political society, consent and hegemony, 'an indispensable concept'; 'flexible positional superiority' (pp. 6–7); 'The same kind of paradox is to be found in Marx'; 'Gramsci, certainly, and Foucault and Raymond Williams in their very different ways' (p. 14); 'unlike Michel Foucault, to whose work I am greatly indebted' and 'the determining imprint of individual writers' (p. 23); 'the whole complex problem of knowledge and power' (p. 24); the Gramscian 'inventory' (p. 25); 'what Michel Foucault calls a discourse, whose material presence or weight' (p. 94); Marx and India (pp. 153–156); 'every European ... was consequently a racist, an imperialist, and almost totally ethnocentric', with the following qualification (p. 204); Bernard Lewis (p. 321). Chapters 1–3 otherwise unread."}, "scope_note": "In-period text of the book, used for pages beyond the introduction transcription already in the graph (said_orientalism)."}
{"id": "pe137_said_question_palestine_1979", "title": "Edward W. Said, The Question of Palestine (New York: Times Books, 1979; Vintage Books, 1980).", "url": "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/159795/the-question-of-palestine-by-edward-w-said/", "citation": "Edward W. Said, The Question of Palestine (New York: Times Books, 1979); copy read: First Vintage Books Edition, October 1980.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Internet Archive item the-question-of-palestine-said-edward (OCR text layer of a scan; imprint: 'First Vintage Books Edition, October 1980 ... Originally published by Times Books ... in November 1979'). Pages are the 1980 Vintage reprint's; whether they match the 1979 Times Books edition was not checked. Read: contents; chapter 2 opening pp. 56–59; pp. 74–76. Passages used: chapter title 'Zionism from the Standpoint of Its Victims'; ideas to be examined 'genealogically' and 'as practical systems for accumulation (of power, land, ideological legitimacy) and displacement (of people, other ideas, prior legitimacy)' (p. 57); 'the great dispossessing movements of modern European colonialism' (p. 76) and lands declared 'open to European white settlers' (p. 76). The rest of the book is unread."}, "scope_note": "Node only (and the settler_colonialism note): the 1979 extension of his argument to Palestine."}
{"id": "pe137_said_reconsidered_1985", "title": "Edward W. Said, “Orientalism Reconsidered”, Cultural Critique 1 (Autumn 1985): 89–107.", "url": "https://doi.org/10.2307/1354282", "citation": "Edward W. Said, “Orientalism Reconsidered”, Cultural Critique 1 (1985): 89–107.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "Read in full in a JSTOR PDF (cover sheet: Cultural Critique No. 1, Autumn 1985, pp. 89–107; stable URL 0882-4371(198523)0:1<89:OR>2.0.CO;2-6) available elsewhere. Crossref record for doi:10.2307/1354282 agrees (Cultural Critique, 1985, first page 89, author Said). Passages used: 'the relationship between power and knowledge' (p. 89); German Orientalism omission 'superficial or trivial'; Dennis Porter's charge 'ahistorical and inconsistent' and 'more weighty in assertion than it is in proof' (p. 90); 'the right of formerly un- or mis-represented human groups to speak for and represent themselves' (p. 91); 'got left out of Orientalism was precisely the very history that resisted'; Geertz's 'self-congratulatory cliches' (p. 94); historicism and 'the science of world history' (Braudel, Wallerstein, Perry Anderson, Wolf) (p. 101); 'theories of accumulation on a world scale ...' and 'the problem is once again historicism' (p. 102); 'a praxis of the same sort ... as male gender dominance, or patriarchy' (p. 103); Subaltern Studies, Spivak and Bhabha among anti-Orientalist projects (pp. 104–105)."}, "scope_note": "In-period essay: his reply to critics and his critique of historicism and world history."}
{"id": "pe137_spivak_cpr_1999", "title": "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, A Critique of Postcolonial Reason: Toward a History of the Vanishing Present (Cambridge, Mass.: Harvard University Press, 1999), chapter 3, “History”.", "url": "https://www.hup.harvard.edu/books/9780674177642", "citation": "Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak, A Critique of Postcolonial Reason (Harvard University Press, 1999), ch. 3, “History” (revised “Can the Subaltern Speak?”); reprinted in Rosalind C. Morris, ed., Can the Subaltern Speak? Reflections on the History of an Idea (Columbia University Press, 2010), part one.", "verification": {"status": "supporting_page_checked", "checked_on": "2026-10-14", "note": "The revised essay was read in the Columbia University Press e-book of Morris (2010), part one, headed '“Can the Subaltern Speak?” revised edition, from the “History” chapter of Critique of Postcolonial Reason'; the e-book has no page numbers. Passages used: 'in the first version of this text, I wrote, in the accents of passionate lament: the subaltern cannot speak! It was an inadvisable remark'; Bhubaneswari Bhaduri 'was not a “true” subaltern'; the revised sati sentence ('imposed upon those women a greater ideological construction ... by an ignorant (but sanctioned) synecdoche'). Rajeswari Sunder Rajan's essay in the same volume gives CPR p. 308 for the 'passionate lament' passage; the printed book was not seen. The rest of the book is unread."}, "scope_note": "In-period (1999) revision of the 1988 essay; her own retrospect on its closing sentence."}

