data/extension-2026/postcolonial-persons-2026-10-14/batch.json:14: "rationale": "Person entries go to thinkers whose frameworks students meet across fields (user, 2026-10-13). Said's Orientalism, Guha's subaltern programme and Spivak's question of subaltern speech are read across postcolonial history, Subaltern Studies, gender, labour and world history. Chakrabarty became a person entry in 1.132 with no incoming edges; Guha and Spivak supply them on read evidence. Each draft's NOTES.md records what was read in full, in part, through reviewers or reprints, and what was not read (among them Ahmad's In Theory, Parry 1987, Clifford 1980, Rule of Property and the 1997 Dominance without Hegemony). In-period critics are kept: al-'Azm, Lewis, Clifford and Ahmad (as reported) on Said; O'Hanlon, Bayly, Sarkar and Spivak herself on Guha; O'Hanlon, Sarkar and Eagleton on Spivak. Dissertation evidence is metadata only and never in entry text.",
