data/extension-2026/postcolonial-persons-2026-10-14/spivak/entry-draft.json:13:  "description": "A literary theorist and the translator of Derrida’s Of Grammatology (1976), whose essays on colonial India became reference points for historians. In ‘Subaltern Studies: Deconstructing Historiography’ (1985) she read the collective ‘from within but against the grain’, proposing that its recovery of subaltern consciousness be read ‘as a strategic use of positivist essentialism in a scrupulously visible political interest’, and argued that it overlooked ‘how important the concept-metaphor woman is’ to its own discourse. ‘Can the Subaltern Speak?’ (1988) separates representation as ‘speaking for’ from ‘re-presentation’, argues that in Foucault and Deleuze’s conversation intellectuals, ‘representing them, ... represent themselves as transparent’, and reads the British abolition of sati through the sentence ‘White men are saving brown women from brown men’, set against the nativist ‘The women actually wanted to die’: between them ‘One never encounters the testimony of the women’s voice-consciousness.’ She later called the essay’s closing ‘the subaltern cannot speak!’ ‘an inadvisable remark’ (1999). In period, Gyan Prakash (1992) held that she ‘very correctly marks the silencing of the subaltern woman’ as a limit of historical understanding, Rosalind O’Hanlon (1988) urged hesitation ‘before accepting’ her proposal to re-inscribe the series’ strategy ‘as a strategy for our times’, and Terry Eagleton (1999) found her Critique ‘too well-mannered, as well as too episodic’ to be the critique its title promised.",
data/extension-2026/postcolonial-persons-2026-10-14/spivak/entry-draft.json:34:  "scope_note": "This entry rests on texts read on 2026-10-14: ‘Can the Subaltern Speak?’ in the 1988 text as reprinted in Williams and Chrisman, Colonial Discourse and Post-Colonial Theory (1993; pp. 66–111, opening abridged), with page numbers of that reprint; two 1988 page anchors (p. 277 and p. 308) come from Chakrabarty (1992, n. 48) and Sunder Rajan (2010). The 1999 revision was read in Morris’s 2010 reprint, which has no page numbers. ‘Subaltern Studies: Deconstructing Historiography’ was read in Subaltern Studies IV (1985), pp. 330–363, in full; ‘Three Women’s Texts’ pp. 243–245; the translator’s preface to Of Grammatology at p. lxxvii of the 1997 corrected edition. She is a literary critic, not a historian, and is not presented as a founder of postcolonial studies; she joined the Subaltern Studies editorial collective in the 1990s (Chaturvedi gives Subaltern Studies IX, 1996; Chatterjee recalls 1993) and in 2000 called herself ‘a sometime member’. Benita Parry’s in-period critique (Oxford Literary Review, 1987), Aijaz Ahmad’s In Theory (1992) and Lata Mani’s work on sati (1987, 1998) were not read; Ahmad appears only through Eagleton’s report of her contrast with him. Chatterjee (2010) is a later reading by a member of the collective and carries no in-period claim."
