data/extension-2026/postcolonial-persons-2026-10-14/said/entry-draft.json:13:  "description": "A Palestinian-American literary critic whose Orientalism (1978) reads European writing on 'the Orient' as 'a Western style for dominating, restructuring, and having authority over the Orient': a discourse in Foucault's sense, durable through what Gramsci called hegemony, though he held, 'unlike Michel Foucault', to 'the determining imprint of individual writers' and denied that the Orient was 'a creation with no corresponding reality'. The Question of Palestine (1979) examined 'Zionism from the Standpoint of Its Victims', Covering Islam (1981) took up American reporting on Islam, and 'Orientalism Reconsidered' (1985) traced Orientalism to historicism, including the 'science of world history'. Culture and Imperialism (1993) added what Orientalism had 'left out', resistance to empire, and proposed reading the metropolitan canon 'contrapuntally, with a simultaneous awareness both of the metropolitan history that is narrated and of those other histories against which (and together with which) the dominating discourse acts'. In period, Bernard Lewis charged him with 'very arbitrary decisions' of scope, Sadik al-'Azm called his account of Marx 'a travesty', and a 1996 review summarised feminist critics as holding that critics of Orientalism 'continue to write out gender'; Said replied that it was 'idiotic to say that Orientalism is a conspiracy'.",
data/extension-2026/postcolonial-persons-2026-10-14/said/entry-draft.json:35:  "scope_note": "A literary critic, not a historian; the entry is for a framework historians meet across fields. It rests on texts read on 2026-10-14: Orientalism in the 1978 Pantheon first edition (introduction pp. 1–28 in full; pp. 94, 153–156, 204 and 321 for passages used); The Question of Palestine (1980 Vintage reprint of the 1979 book; pp. 56–59 and 74–76); the Covering Islam introduction (p. ix); 'Orientalism Reconsidered' (1985) in full; the foreword to Selected Subaltern Studies (1988, pp. v–x) in full; and Culture and Imperialism (1993 Knopf first edition; introduction pp. xii–xxv and pp. 41–42, 51, 66, 240–245, 251 and 278). Not read: 'Traveling Theory' (The World, the Text, and the Critic, 1983) and 'Foucault and the Imagination of Power' (1986), both lending-only or closed here; his critique of Foucault is taken from Culture and Imperialism, which cites the 1986 essay as 'my critique'. He is not presented as a founder: a 1992 critique calls his blend of themes 'virtually a paradigm for a new generation of historians and anthropologists' (O'Hanlon and Washbrook, first page only) and a 1996 review calls Orientalism 'undoubtedly' the inspiration behind critical interest in 'empire, knowledge and power' (McEwan); these are readers' judgements. Contested in period by Lewis (1982) and al-'Azm (1981), both read; James Clifford's review (History and Theory, 1980), Lata Mani and Ruth Frankenberg (Economy and Society, 1985), Dennis Porter (1983) and Aijaz Ahmad (1991–92) are recorded but were not read: Porter is known here from Said's 1985 reply, Ahmad from Robert Irwin's later account (2006), Reina Lewis (1996) from McEwan's review. Works after 2000 are not drawn on; Irwin (2006) is a labelled later reading."
